  "response": "Whether the heart drives itself or is driven by the nervous system was settled through converging comparative, embryological, anatomical and clinical evidence gathered over more than a century. The modern myogenic position took shape when Walter Gaskell argued in the 1880s that the vertebrate heartbeat originates in the cardiac muscle. The grounds for that claim were experimental: Gaskell based his arguments partly on experiments with the tortoise heart. In that preparation, slow conduction let him watch excitation pass from chamber to chamber through muscular tissue, which supported his conclusion that the muscle itself initiates and propagates the beat.\n\nOther laboratories tested the question with different species and methods. Theodor Engelmann supported the myogenic theory using experiments on frog hearts, whereas Wilhelm His Jr. reported that the embryonic heart beats before nerve fibers grow into it. The embryological observation indicates that a heart cut off from all extrinsic nerves continues to beat rhythmically, because the early embryo supplies a heart that has never been connected to those nerves. In other words, removing every external nerve connection to the heart does not stop its regular beating. Developmental timing therefore matched what surgical denervation in adult animals had already suggested.\n\nComparative physiology complicated the picture, since a neurogenic heart clearly existed elsewhere in the animal kingdom. Although Anton Carlson demonstrated in 1904 that the heartbeat of the horseshoe crab Limulus is neurogenic, the vertebrate evidence pointed the other way: because the denervated heart keeps its rhythm, the human heartbeat is myogenic in origin. Carlson's crab nonetheless kept the opposing hypothesis formally alive. As a result, physiologists framed the human case as exhaustive. Either the myogenic account holds for humans or the human heartbeat is neurogenic in origin. The two cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. The physiological community later determined that the first of these alternatives, the myogenic account, is the one that holds for humans. Treating the problem as a strict choice gave every subsequent observation a definite bearing on it.\n\nAnatomical work next sought the specific tissue in which a myogenic rhythm would begin. Physiologists reasoned that a self-exciting heart required a localized region of modified muscle with pacemaker properties. Keith and Flack discovered the sinoatrial node in 1920, whereas Sunao Tawara was the first to describe the sinoatrial node, having traced specialized muscular conduction tissue in careful serial histological sections of the mammalian heart. These identifications gave the theory a concrete substrate. A discrete band of specialized myocardium, rather than a nerve plexus, showed the structural features expected of the origin of the beat, and its location matched the site where physiologists had recorded the earliest electrical activity.\n\nCombining these lines of evidence, the physiological community ultimately concluded that the human heartbeat is myogenic in origin. That conclusion settles the tension Carlson's work had introduced: the neurogenic Limulus heart became a recognized invertebrate exception rather than a model for vertebrates. Cardiac transplantation later supplied a clinical test, because the donor heart is implanted with its extrinsic nerves severed. At least one of two intrinsic sources must set the pace of such an organ, perhaps both. Either the rhythm of a transplanted human heart is driven by its sinoatrial node, or it is driven by intrinsic cardiac ganglia. The ganglionic mechanism was considered before electrophysiological studies concluded that the sinoatrial node drives the rhythm of the transplanted human heart. Those recordings tied the transplanted heart's steady, autonomous rate to the same nodal tissue that anatomists had described. The transplanted heart thus reproduces, in a living patient, the observation that began with isolated animal preparations: the human heart generates its own rhythm.",
